00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.840
absolutely I I adore that. I love that

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.479
part of GitHub sponsors. Uh, but I would

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:52.720
love to see larger corporations step up

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.560
and put more money where they're driving

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:57.280
value from.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.960
>> So, you think that the most sustainable

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.319
model would be basically the

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:03.199
corporations uh funding other nonprofits

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:04.720
standing behind those projects that

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.879
would be then able to pay maintainers so

00:08:06.879 --> 00:08:08.639
they don't have to work in their free

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:10.960
time and [snorts] weekends.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.960
>> That would be awesome. Um, that said,

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.039
like the company I work for is Century,

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:17.440
and Sentry goes really far in trying to

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.639
fund open source maintainers. Uh, the

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:22.639
there's some I forget the name of their

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:25.520
uh campaign, but they're trying to get

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.160
corporations on board in spending a

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.240
significant chunk of money each year.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.880
And I think Centuries is over 100,000

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.039
per year, which is they're trying to

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.200
make a difference, basically a software

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.560
salary to to make that work. So you

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.120
maintain lots of different projects and

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.839
part of it is also working with people

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:49.760
>> and uh in one uh in one conversation one

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.760
you once you said sometimes you just

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.680
have to close the issue move on and try

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.480
not to take it to heart

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.560
>> unfortunately I'm not very good about

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.200
that. Uh I think there's a psychological

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.800
>> you get angry messages with people

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.279
because you closed their issue or you

00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:06.800
