to start contributing to open source?

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.800
How do how do I find a project that's

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.480
suitable for me?

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.240
>> I love this question. This is one of my

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.240
favorite things to answer on my YouTube

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.920
and stream. I think I it's the most

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.399
common question I get as well. What I

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:56.800
would recommend for new projects, if you

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.720
want to get into open source, the first

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.040
place to look is tools that you use and

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.519
love. And I'm sure even the tools you

00:12:03.519 --> 00:12:05.200
use and love once in a while you'll find

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.440
something where you're like that's not

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.760
quite how I would want it to work or

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:11.760
this thing could be better or maybe you

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.600
find a typo in a help message. Like it

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:15.600
could be something extremely small or

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:17.680
extremely large. U start with the

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.839
projects that you know and love though.

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:22.639
And the other thing you can look for is

00:12:22.639 --> 00:12:24.399
in those projects, they will often have

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:26.480
issues that are tagged like easy for

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:29.600
newcomers or something that can help you

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.920
get started and figure out contributing

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.480
and jumping in to fix something or it

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:36.079
could be something as simple as triaging

00:12:36.079 --> 00:12:38.639
issues. I know the CPython project has

00:12:38.639 --> 00:12:41.279
official triagers and uh we had a

00:12:41.279 --> 00:12:44.639
keynote earlier that was very helpful in

00:12:44.639 --> 00:12:46.480
